[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"EVM Opcodes, Gas Optimization, and Metering in Solidity"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Understanding the Ethereum Virtual Machine (EVM) opcodes and how gas metering functions is essential for writing high-performance, low-cost smart contracts in Solidity. Every operation in the EVM has a specific gas cost associated with it, designed to prevent infinite loops and allocate resource usage fairly."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. How EVM Gas Metering Works"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The EVM executes bytecode instruction by instruction. Each instruction corresponds to an opcode (such as ADD, PUSH1, SLOAD, SSTORE). Execution stops when the program counter reaches the end of the bytecode, or when an error occurs (such as out-of-gas or an invalid jump)."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Base Cost: Every transaction starts with a flat 21,000 gas fee."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Memory Expansion: Accessing higher memory indices incurs a quadratic cost growth."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Storage Operations: SSTORE and SLOAD are among the most expensive operations due to state persistence."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"2. High-Cost Opcodes to Avoid"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"Storage writes (SSTORE) are heavily metered. Modifying an existing storage slot from a non-zero value to another non-zero value costs 2,900 gas (post-Berlin upgrade), while writing to a zero slot (initialization) costs 20,000 gas. Conversely, clearing storage refunds gas."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
pragma solidity ^0.8.0;

contract GasInefficient {
    uint256 public count;

    // Inefficient: Reads and writes to storage in a loop
    function incrementBy(uint256[] calldata values) external {
        for (uint256 i = 0; i < values.length; i++) {
            count += values[i];
        }
    }
}"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Gas Optimization Techniques in Solidity"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"To minimize gas consumption, developers should leverage memory and stack variables instead of repeatedly hitting storage, pack storage variables into 32-byte slots, and use 'calldata' instead of 'memory' for read-only function parameters."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
pragma solidity ^0.8.0;

contract GasEfficient {
    uint256 public count;

    // Efficient: Uses a local memory variable to accumulate before writing to storage once
    function incrementBy(uint256[] calldata values) external {
        uint256 localCount = count;
        uint256 length = values.length;

        for (uint256 i = 0; i < length;) {
            localCount += values[i];
            unchecked { i++; }
        }

        count = localCount;
    }
}"},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use 'unchecked { i++; }' in loops to bypass overflow checks when an overflow is mathematically impossible."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Cache array lengths outside loops to avoid repetitive MLOAD opcodes."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use custom errors instead of long string require statements to save deployment and execution bytecode size."}]
